      "math_academy_id": "q-73907",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & -1 & -3 \\\\  &  & -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+1)(0-1)(0+3) \\\\  & =(1)(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-1,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/40b0fc54d72820b2893dba5160b4e5f1c70bf7b63aebd63eb3d5ea0ad078dcef.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
